e and ring that little bell so  that you  never miss out on a video okay so let's  kick things off with the first  diet food that i ever encountered as a  young girl grapefruit  so grapefruit to me could pretty much be  the north american mascot for  diet food and that my friends can be  traced back to a popular 1930s diet  called the grapefruit diet the diet  claimed that you could lose 10 to 12  pounds within 12 days  and relied on the unique enzymes in  grapefruit  to help allegedly burn away fat  this has a special place in my heart  because the grapefruit diet was actually  the first official diet  that i ever went on in grade 10 because  my mom was doing some version of it and  my friend also wanted to try it  well let's just say with only 800  calories a day  i didn't even last a full 24 hours so  yeah  i didn't lose any weight i just got  really hangry the truth is that  grapefruit is a really healthy food  packed with vitamin c  beta-carotene lycopene antioxidants and  flavinones  it also has just a hundred calories and  four grams of fiber in one  whole fruit plus a whole cup of water  having said that it's not a magic weight  loss food that  burns away calories from other foods one  study analyzing the effects of daily  consumption of grapefruit on body weight  and healthy  overweight adults showed that while  grapefruit does have a number of  health benefits related to heart disease  prevention  there were actually no significant  results relating grapefruit consumption  to weight loss specifically i think  grapefruit is fantastic and would be a  great low calorie high fiber nutrient  dense food to add to  any weight loss diet plan if you enjoy  it but it's  not a magic pill so some ways that you  can enjoy grapefruit would be  with some nuts as a snack as part of  your breakfast with some nut butter and  toast  or thrown into a beautiful colorful  salad at lunch  so for its inherent nutrient content  alone  i'm gonna give grapefruit a 9 out of 10.  i honestly can't really fault it for  much other than the fact that it really  won't keep you  that state-shaded for very long next  let's talk about  rice cakes which if you are making a  comeback just judging by  how many people's wedding day videos are  featuring them  but our love of rice cakes really  started back in the 90s when the world  became obsessed  with low fat and low calorie snack foods  and as we've recently seen the pendulum  swing in the opposite direction  we're now seeing them make an appearance  as a vessel to carry  high fat foods like nut butter and  smashed avocado  but let's break down the facts so  rice cakes have only around 35 calories  zero fat and around seven grams of carbs  each so  yes i can see why they're among the top  choice  for dieters and even those in the  fitness community who are counting carbs  as well  but they're free of fiber and free of  protein and you know i'm a huge fan of  snacks with the hunger crushing  compounds  fat protein and fiber rice cakes  unfortunately have  zero of these three they're also not a  major source of any vitamins and  minerals so  translation well even if you're using  these to kind of fill you up for a few  calories  they're not actually going to keep you  full for very long  having said that i actually personally  really like  rice cakes and i'll tell you why for one  there's just something satisfying about  that strange  foamy like crunch especially the  flavored ones that are like  coated in that salt and vinegar  seasoning i legit  lived on those when i had morning  sickness this time around so  i get it i mean if you like them  flavored or otherwise  great i think they're an easy snackable  inexpensive way to get your crunchy figs  but i mainly think of them as like an  easy vessel for  nutrition and for like a spread that  would be more rich  in those hunger crushing compounds that  we just talked about so some ideas on  how to serve a rice cake would be  as a snack top with some avocado and  hemp hearts or  natural peanut butter and some chia jam  or  for a meal you could top it off with  some egg or tuna salad  so i would give rice cakes like a six  and a half out of ten on their own  and a solid eight out of ten when paired  with some more nutritious  dense foods next let's talk about  cottage cheese  the og diet dairy that has since been  eclipsed by greek yogurt in popularity  now north america's obsession with  cottage cheese  actually began in the 1950s and then  peaked in the early 1970s  when the average american ate around  five pounds of it  per year that my friends is a lot of  curds  but one cup of plain two percent cottage  cheese  packs in an impressive 30 grams of  protein  along with calcium phosphorus and  vitamin b12  it's also naturally a low fat cheese so  even though you can find zero one two  and four percent options most readily on  the market  they're all still relatively low calorie  low fat options  we're talking the difference between  like 120 calories  and zero to five grams of fat per half  cup  not really a big deal in my books  there's also lactose free options on the  market for those who can't easily  tolerate dairy so  if that sounds like you try that one out  too the only thing to flag about cottage  cheese  especially as it compares to other high  protein dairy like  greek or obscure yogurt is that it is  naturally high in sodium almost 20  percent of your daily needs in just a  half cup  so if you are sensitive to salt it's  something to watch and maybe keep an eye  on your intake for the rest of the  day or you can try to find a low sodium  option  which only contains around three percent  of your daily salt  so my favorite ways to enjoy cottage  cheese is in a parfait with berries and  nuts  pureed and spread on toast like ricotta  would be with some bananas on top  pureed with some frozen fruit to make my  two ingredient froyo  or pureed into pancake batter to make  protein pancakes  i will leave some links below to some of  my go-to recipes  but i'm going to give cos cheese a 9 out  of 10.  okay now on the topic of breakfast  parfaits  let's talk about granola so here's a  little weak factoid that you can bust  out at your next dinner party  granola was apparently a thing as far  back as the late 1800s  a man named dr james c jackson developed  a cereal like mixture  from gram flour which he named granula  which sounds a lot like how my kid would  probably pronounce granola  but then a decade later seventh-day  adventist director dr  john harvey kellogg created a blend  composed of baked  and re-baked whole grains which he ended  up calling granola  only because he was going to get sued if  he used granula  but anyways a century later granola was  adopted by the modern health food  movement in the 1960s  and to this day is often still called a  hippie health food  again thanks to wellness culture this is  coming full circle  now granola is admittedly kind of hard  to assess from a nutritional standpoint  as there are endless types of varieties  and different ingredients  but let's look at a more mainstream  budget friendly granola store brand  and one that you would find in more  specialty stores  so our grocery store granola is made  with whole grain oat flakes  sugar canola oil and palm oil honey  sunflower seeds and flavor our premium  brand is made with organic gluten-free  oats  organic coconut sugar organic raw virgin  coconut oil  organic sunflower seeds organic puffed  amaranth  organic quinoa flakes organic chia seeds  organic cinnamon  and salt the good news is in both the  first ingredient is  oats and food labeling laws require that  food manufacturers list  ingredients in order of volume meaning  that the first ingredient is present in  the greatest amount in that food product  and the last ingredient is usually found  in very small amounts  i'm not going to get into the organic  difference between these two i mean  because that's a whole preference and  that is a whole other video but  generally you will see some themes here  oats is number one  sugar is the second ingredient in both  of these  some kind of oil is the third and the  rest  is flavors or other nuts seeds or grains  nutrition wise the premium actually has  a lot more calories  fat salt carb and sugar but it also has  more fiber and protein  so it does look like the premium brand  just has more of these nutritious  add-ins which could account for some of  the extra calories  fiber protein and fat and this just goes  to show that lower calorie doesn't  always mean  more nutrient dense and satiating but  we're looking at somewhere between  300 and 450 calories per cup 5 to 20  grams of sugar  5 to 10 grams of protein and 5 to 7  grams of fiber  which you choose would really depend on  your unique health goals  taste preference concern about  ingredient quality like organic or  gluten-free or non-gmo  and of course budget now when it comes  to  cereal the general recommendation is to  look for one with at least 4 grams of  fiber  less than 10 grams of sugar less than  three grams of fat  and less than 360 milligrams of sodium  per serving  so a store-bought granola may meet these  criteria  but as we've seen it also very well may  not  so if you're having a hard time finding  a granola that meets your unique needs  you can easily just make one yourself at  home  i actually have an awesome recipe for  espresso granola in the mindful glow  cookbook which  i'm going to link below but with its  whole grains  nuts and seeds i think that granola is  typically a pretty nutrient-dense food  and because it is more nutrient-dense  and has more calories more sugar more  fat  than let's say a typical breakfast  cereal like all bran or cheerios  you likely aren't going to eat quite as  much  so some ways that i love to eat granola  is to throw it on top of greek yogurt  or if you want to add some nice texture  and flavor to a less exciting breakfast  cereal like bran flakes  you could mix the two together and then  serve with milk  and ideally a milk that's a little  higher in protein like soy  cows or pea it's also delicious as a  handheld snack milk with some popcorn  for an on the go little thing  but i would give granola on average  acknowledging that there are huge  differences  in brands and even if you make it at  home  a generic 6.5 out of 10 strictly from a  nutritional perspective  and let's finish things off with  everyone's favorite  wobbly snack jello so jello has been a  popular american dessert since the late  1800s  yet somehow jello shifted from our  dessert menus  to our diet plans sometime in the 20th  century with the so-called  jello diet proof that you can make a new  popular weight loss diet  simply by limiting the number of foods  that you eat to a narrow selection  anyways proponents of this diet claim  that you can lose 10 to 20 pounds in a  matter of  weeks but let's take a look so jello is  made out of sugar  gelatin aka a protein extracted from the  skin ligaments and bones of animals  artificial flavors some preservatives  and of course  colors jello became a popular diet food  again during the low fat craze  since it is officially completely  fat-free  and in the world of desserts it's also  relatively low in calories with  one serving clocking in at just 80  calories  thankfully we have come a long way since  the 80s  and we now know that fat free doesn't  automatically mean  health food as one serving contains four  and a half  teaspoons of added sugar and not much  else so that means  no fiber not much protein and no  important vitamins or minerals  we also now have sugar-free jell-o which  is sweetened with aspartame  but it still doesn't provide anything  additive nutrition wise  it is also important to briefly touch on  the fact that certain food dyes  contain chemicals that have been linked  to hyperactivity in some children  or allergies in others and one dye in  particular  red 3 has been linked in animal research  to thyroid tumors  jell-o uses red 40 not red 3  as do most red colored food products  today so this is  unlikely to be a huge health concern  having  said that for a lot of people jello is  nostalgic and  is easy to eat especially for those on  texture restrictive diets  and that alone is reason enough to enjoy  it from time to time  and in the grand scheme of desserts  which are not meant to be health foods  in my opinion  just kind of meant to be enjoyed it is a  low calorie option that might satisfy  your mouth  and emotional hunger for something sweet  if it brings you joy  amazing jello it up but if we want to  enjoy jello in a more balanced way that  could boost the nutrition content a bit  we could set the jello with some fresh  fruit or greek yogurt in it  or make your own using fruit or veggie  juice  on its own however speaking purely from  a nutritional standpoint  i would give jello a two out of ten in  conclusion i want to reiterate that  there are no good or bad  foods just more or less nutritious ones  and part of healthy eating is balancing  nutrition with pleasure  but eating any one of these foods that i  ranked highly will not make you healthy  nor will eating any that i did not rank  as favorably  make you unhealthy all of these foods  can  and do fit in a healthy diet if they're  enjoyable for you  so consider all of this information just  another  data point and nothing else and that is  all for today folks i wanted to keep  things sweet and short  but i'd love to know if you liked this  style of video do you appreciate the  more  nutrition heavy information does this  help you make decisions about maybe some  new foods to try to incorporate your  diet  does this give you permission to not eat  certain foods if they don't bring you  joy  let me know by giving this video the  thumbs up if you liked it  leave me a comment below about this  video style or about other classic diet  foods that you'd like to see me review  don't forget to subscribe to this  channel and i'll see you next time on  abby's kitchen  bye

